## Claude Code

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/claude-code.png" name="Claude Code" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic's own coding agent. Its docs describe it as "an agentic coding tool that reads your codebase, edits files, runs commands, and integrates with your development tools," available in the terminal, IDE extensions, a desktop app and the browser. The terminal CLI is the full-featured surface; VS Code (including Cursor) and JetBrains extensions, the desktop app and the web are the other ways in. As of November 26, 2026, the public `anthropics/claude-code` repository has 157,195 GitHub stars (GitHub API, checked that day), and GitHub reports no license on it, so we list it as not open source.

**Who it is for.** Developers who are comfortable driving an agent from a terminal, want durable project instructions that follow the repo, and want hooks that can block or rewrite actions deterministically. It is also the natural pick if you already pay for Claude, because Claude Code is part of the Pro, Max and Ultra plans rather than a separate product line.

**Key features (from Anthropic's docs).**

- **CLAUDE.md memory.** Plain markdown files loaded at the start of every session, at user, project and managed-policy scope, with `@path` imports and a private `CLAUDE.local.md`. The docs recommend keeping each file under 200 lines.
- **Auto memory.** Notes Claude writes itself from your corrections, loaded from the first 200 lines or 25KB of `MEMORY.md`.
- **Path-scoped rules** in `.claude/rules/`, so instructions only load when matching files are in play.
- **Skills.** A folder with a `SKILL.md`, invoked automatically from its description or by typing `/skill-name`. Anthropic says Claude Code skills follow the Agent Skills open standard and adds extras such as invocation control and subagent execution.
- **Subagents** that run in their own context window with their own tool access and return only a summary.
- **Hooks**, which are user-defined shell commands run at lifecycle points, for deterministic control rather than hoping the model remembers.
- **MCP** connections to external tools, databases and APIs.
- **Remote Control** (announced by @claudeai on February 24, 2026): start a task in the terminal and continue it from your phone while the session keeps running on your machine.

{% x id="2026418433911603668" /%}

- **Computer use in the CLI** (announced March 30, 2026 as a research preview on Pro and Max): Claude can open apps, click through a UI and test what it built.

{% x id="2038663014098899416" /%}

**Pros**

- Memory, rules, hooks, skills and subagents are all first-class and documented together, so team conventions travel with the repo.
- One subscription covers chat and the coding agent; the usage pool is shared across Claude on web, desktop, mobile and Claude Code.
- Reads `AGENTS.md` directly when no `CLAUDE.md` is present (needs Claude Code v2.1.277 or later), so a repo set up for other agents works without changes.
- Works from the terminal, so it fits SSH sessions, CI-style scripting and any editor.

**Cons**

- No free plan: Anthropic's pricing table shows Claude Code as "No" on Free.
- Anthropic models only through the subscription, so you cannot mix in other vendors' models the way you can in an editor-centric tool.
- Instructions in CLAUDE.md are context, not enforcement. Anthropic's own docs say to use a PreToolUse hook if you need to block an action regardless of what the model decides.
- Because all Claude surfaces draw from one pool, a heavy chat day can eat into your coding budget.

**Benefits for your use case.** If your pain is "the agent forgets how our repo works," CLAUDE.md plus `.claude/rules/` plus repo-committed skills is the most complete documented answer among the tools in this post. If your pain is "I want to review every diff in a polished editor," you will probably use Claude Code's VS Code extension or pair it with an editor.

**Pricing (claude.com/pricing, checked November 26, 2026; excludes tax).**

| Plan | Price | What the page says |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0 | No Claude Code |
| Pro | $17 per month billed annually ($200 up front), or $20 monthly | Includes Claude Code; usage limits every 5 hours and every week |
| Max | $100 per month | 5x Pro usage every 5 hours and every week; $100 a month in API credits |
| Ultra | $200 per month | No 5-hour limit for verified accounts; 10x Pro usage every week; $200 a month in API credits |

Team and Enterprise plans also exist (Team is listed for teams of 2 to 150); see the pricing page for current seat prices, which we did not capture reliably. Anthropic states that prices and plans are subject to change.

**Free tier / trial.** None for Claude Code. You can use Claude's free chat plan, but Claude Code requires a paid plan or an Anthropic Console account (docs: "Most surfaces require a Claude subscription or Anthropic Console account").

**Limits.** Most accounts have a rolling five-hour window, and paid plans add weekly limits on top. Anthropic does not publish a fixed token or message count per plan, only relative multiples (1x, 5x, 10x weekly).

**Standout use case.** A monorepo where different directories need different instructions: a root `CLAUDE.md`, nested ones loaded on demand, `.claude/rules/` scoped by file type, and a hook that formats on every edit.

**Skip it if** you want a free plan, you need to switch freely between model vendors, or you would rather live in a graphical editor than a terminal or side panel.

**Install and setup.** Anthropic's docs list a native installer as the recommended path, plus Homebrew and WinGet:

Homebrew on macOS or Linux, WinGet on Windows, then run `claude` inside a repo:

```bash
brew install --cask claude-code
winget install Anthropic.ClaudeCode
claude
```

Run `/init` once to have Claude generate a starting `CLAUDE.md` from your codebase. A minimal project file looks like this:

```markdown
## Commands
- Test: `npm test`
- Lint: `npm run lint`
## Conventions
- TypeScript strict mode; no default exports.
- Ask before adding a production dependency.
```

{% figure src="https://ptht05hbb1ssoooe.public.blob.vercel-storage.com/assets/og/opengraph-default.png" alt="Cursor default social image from cursor.com" caption="Cursor's default site image. Source: Cursor (cursor.com)" /%}

## Cursor

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/cursor.png" name="Cursor" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** An AI code editor and agent platform. Its docs describe Agent as "Cursor's assistant that can complete complex coding tasks independently, run terminal commands, and edit code," and the product also includes a CLI, cloud agents, automations and Bugbot code review. In April 2026 Cursor announced Cursor 3 as "built for a world where all code is written by agents, while keeping the depth of a development environment."

{% x id="2039768512894505086" /%}

**The ownership and pricing change you need to know.** On August 14, 2026 Cursor's blog said the company "has officially been acquired by SpaceX," completing a process that began in April with a partnership with SpaceXAI. Cursor's help center now says Cursor is "part of The X Subscription, one subscription for Grok, Cursor, and X," that new customers choose a plan there, and that anyone who subscribed before it launched is on a legacy Cursor plan that "keeps working as it is." The practical effect: the plan names and prices you see in older reviews (Pro $20, Pro+ $60) are legacy, and the current public pricing page leads with Free, Plus, Premium, Super and Ultra.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want an editor as home base, teams that want code hosting, cloud agents and AI review under one admin console, and people who want to choose among several model vendors in one place.

**Key features (from Cursor's docs and pricing page).**

- **Agents in the editor and the CLI.** The CLI (`agent`) supports Agent, Plan and Ask modes, a print mode (`agent -p "..."`) for scripts and CI, session resume, a sandbox setting, and an `&` prefix that hands the conversation to a Cloud Agent.
- **Cloud agents and automations**, **Bugbot** AI code reviews and **Origin** code hosting, all listed from the Premium plan up.
- **Rules**: `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` project rules with `description`, `globs` and `alwaysApply` frontmatter, plus User Rules, Team Rules and `AGENTS.md` (root and nested).
- **Agent Skills**: `SKILL.md` folders in `.cursor/skills/` or `.agents/skills/`, and for compatibility Cursor also loads `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/`.
- **Hooks** via `hooks.json`, with a documented path for loading hooks from third-party tools such as Claude Code.
- **MCP** over stdio, SSE and Streamable HTTP, configured in `mcp.json` or from the Customize page, with a Cursor Marketplace for plugins.
- **Models**: Cursor's own pool covers Grok 4.7, Grok 4.6, Grok 4.5 and Composer 2.5; third-party models are available on Premium and above at API list price, paid from prepaid credit.

Two posts for context on the model side: Cursor's own Composer 2.5 announcement (May 18, 2026), and a post from the @SpaceXSI account (September 21, 2026) saying Grok 4.7 is available in Cursor, Grok Build and the Grok API.

{% x id="2056415413077233983" /%}

{% x id="2102069822288720022" /%}

**Pros**

- You can see and edit code while the agent works, in an editor built around that loop.
- Third-party models (on Premium and above) sit beside Cursor's own, in one tool.
- Team features that Claude Code's pricing page does not list: a team marketplace for internal rules, skills and plugins, Team Rules with an enforce toggle, SSO and, on Enterprise, SCIM and audit logs.
- Skills written for Claude Code or Codex are picked up from their usual folders, so you are not locked into one layout.

**Cons**

- The pricing and ownership picture changed this year, and it is easy to land on the wrong plan: the cheapest paid tier, Plus at $8, allows Grok models only, with no Auto, Cloud Agents, Automations, Bugbot, Origin or third-party models.
- Third-party model usage is "not included in the monthly prices shown and must be purchased separately", so total cost depends on what you run.
- The pricing page now bundles X and Grok perks (checkmark, ads, X Money rates) into the plan comparison, which is noise if you only want an editor.
- Cursor's rules format (`.mdc`) is proprietary; plain `.md` files in `.cursor/rules` are ignored, which trips people up.

**Benefits for your use case.** If you want to keep your editor, run several agents, and have a team admin view of rules and spend, Cursor is built for that. If you are a solo developer who mostly wants the strongest single model, check whether the Grok-plus-Composer pool is what you want before paying for Premium.

**Pricing (cursor.com/pricing, rendered and checked November 26, 2026; excludes tax).**

| Plan | Monthly | Annual | Usage |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0 | n/a | Limited |
| Plus | $8 | $80 per year | Base, Grok models only |
| Premium | $30 | $300 per year | 4x Plus |
| Super | $100 | $1,000 per year | 15x Plus |
| Ultra | $200 | $2,000 per year | 50x Plus |
| Teams | $30+ per user | not listed | Marketplace for internal rules, skills and plugins |
| Enterprise | Custom | n/a | Pooled usage |

Legacy plans for existing subscribers (Cursor help center): Pro $20, Pro+ $60, Ultra $200, Teams Standard $40 per user, Teams Premium $120 per user. They are "not available to new customers."

**Free tier.** Yes. Cursor's help center says Free includes Agent, Chat and Tab completions with Cursor models (Grok and Composer), with limited usage.

**Limits.** Each paid plan has a weekly pool of usage for Cursor models, shared across Cursor and Grok. Third-party models draw from prepaid credit instead.

**Standout use case.** A team that wants cloud agents opening pull requests, Bugbot reviewing them, and a shared marketplace of rules and skills, all managed from one place.

**Skip it if** you want one flat price for a single vendor's top models, or you do not want your editor subscription tied to X and Grok accounts.

**Setup.** The CLI installer from Cursor's docs:

The install command below is for macOS, Linux and WSL; afterwards run `agent` in a repo:

```bash
curl https://cursor.com/install -fsS | bash
agent "refactor the auth module to use JWT tokens"
```

A project rule that only loads for React components (format taken from Cursor's rules docs):

```markdown
---
description: React component conventions
globs: src/components/**/*.tsx
alwaysApply: false
---
- Use function components and hooks.
- Co-locate tests next to the component.
```

## How skills, rules and AGENTS.md work in each tool

This is the part most comparisons skip, and it decides how painful it is to use more than one agent on the same repository. Every tool below reads some kind of persistent project instruction file, and most now read `SKILL.md` folders. The details differ, so here is what each vendor's documentation says (all pages read November 26, 2026).

| Tool | Always-on instructions | Scoped rules | Skills (`SKILL.md`) | Reads `AGENTS.md`? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Claude Code | `CLAUDE.md` at user, project and managed-policy scope, plus `CLAUDE.local.md` | `.claude/rules/` | `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, personal, nested, enterprise and plugin locations | Yes, but only when no `CLAUDE.md` is found (v2.1.277 or later), unless you import it |
| Cursor | `AGENTS.md` or User Rules and Team Rules | `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` with `globs` and `alwaysApply` | `.cursor/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`, and also `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/` | Yes, root and nested |
| Codex | `AGENTS.md` chain from `~/.codex` down to the working directory, with `AGENTS.override.md` | Nested `AGENTS.md` files | Not checked in this post | Yes, it is the native format |
| Windsurf | `AGENTS.md`, `AGENTS.local.md`, `AGENT.md` | `.devin/rules/` and legacy `.windsurf/rules/` | `.devin/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`, legacy `.windsurf/skills/` | Yes |
| GitHub Copilot | `.github/copilot-instructions.md` | `.github/instructions/NAME.instructions.md` | Not checked in this post | Yes, nearest file wins; also a root `CLAUDE.md` or `GEMINI.md` |
| Gemini CLI | `GEMINI.md` hierarchy (`~/.gemini/GEMINI.md`, workspace files) | Just-in-time and parent-directory context files | `.gemini/skills/` or the `.agents/skills/` alias, user and workspace tiers | Not checked (docs describe `GEMINI.md`) |
| Kiro | Steering files in `.kiro/steering/` and `~/.kiro/steering/` | Steering files included when their description matches | Steering "works similarly to skills" per Kiro's docs | Yes (AGENTS.md support listed) |
| Zed | Not checked | Not checked | Not checked | Not checked |

Three practical consequences follow from those docs.

**1. One `AGENTS.md` is the closest thing to a shared file.** Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Copilot and Kiro all list `AGENTS.md` support. Claude Code reads it too, with one catch: Anthropic's docs say that if a `CLAUDE.md` exists in your working directory or above, Claude reads the `CLAUDE.md` files only. The documented fix is a one-line import:

```markdown
@AGENTS.md

## Claude Code only
- Use the /verify skill before finishing a task.
```

**2. `.claude/skills/` is read by more than Claude Code.** Cursor's skills documentation says it loads skills from `.claude/skills/`, `.codex/skills/`, `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` "for compatibility." So a skills folder committed under `.claude/skills/` already works in both tools in this post. Windsurf, Gemini CLI and Cursor all read `.agents/skills/` as well. In the Claude Code pages we read, the project location is `.claude/skills/`; we did not find `.agents/skills/` listed there, so symlink if you want one folder to serve everything.

**3. Rules are not enforcement.** Anthropic's memory docs say Claude treats CLAUDE.md as context, "not enforced configuration," and point to a PreToolUse hook when you must block an action. Cursor has a comparable hooks system (`preToolUse`, `beforeShellExecution`, `beforeMCPExecution` and others, defined in `hooks.json`) and documents loading hooks from third-party tools like Claude Code. If a rule matters for safety, make it a hook, not a sentence in a markdown file.

Skills as a format are moving toward shared governance. A post from the @ClaudeDevs account on October 22, 2026 said "Your skills keep working, and the licenses don't change," and named engineers from Anthropic, Earendil (Pi), Google, Microsoft, OpenAI and OpenCode as a Technical Steering Committee for the standard.

{% x id="2113328528536305718" /%}

For a deeper look at how these instruction formats differ, see our existing post [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules).

## Other alternatives worth considering

Six more tools came up in the demand data and the market scan. They get shorter sections, with the same pricing discipline: only numbers we read on the vendor's page on November 26, 2026.

### Codex

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/codex.png" name="Codex" size=40 /%}

{% figure src="https://images.ctfassets.net/kftzwdyauwt9/2Z64goaCYuEmGe825kLJFB/ccd8ea0a41e98a3a273357ae621dfe72/codex-og-image.png?w=1600&h=900&fit=fill" alt="OpenAI Codex product social image" caption="Codex product image. Source: OpenAI (openai.com/codex)" /%}

**What it is.** OpenAI's coding agent, available as a CLI, an IDE extension, a desktop app and in the cloud. The CLI is open source under Apache 2.0, and the `openai/codex` repository has 136,569 GitHub stars as of November 26, 2026.

**Who it is for.** People already on ChatGPT who want an agent that shares their plan, and teams that want an open-source CLI they can inspect.

**Pricing (developers.openai.com/codex/pricing, November 26, 2026).** Codex is included in ChatGPT Free, Go, Plus, Pro, Business, Edu and Enterprise plans, and shares pricing, credits and limits with ChatGPT Work. Free is $0 with GPT-6 Luna at Standard speed in the desktop app, "subject to rollout." Plus adds Codex on the web, CLI, IDE extension and iOS, plus cloud integrations like automatic code review and Slack. Pro comes at $100, $200 or $500 a month, with Ultrafast mode on the $500 tier. API-key use is billed separately at API prices and has no cloud features. We did not capture the Plus monthly price from the page, so we leave it out.

**Limits.** OpenAI publishes estimates of local messages per five hours for Plus and Standard Business, for example 17 to 180 for GPT-6.2 Sol, and says these "are not fixed message limits." Pro plans currently have no five-hour limit.

**Pros**
- Free access to try it, and an open-source CLI.
- A well-documented `AGENTS.md` chain, with a global file, per-directory overrides and a default 32 KiB cap on combined size.
- Ties into a ChatGPT plan you may already have.

**Cons**
- Usage is shared with ChatGPT, and message estimates vary by model and task.
- API-key sessions lose cloud features.
- Plan names and models change often, so check the page before buying.

**Benefits.** If your team writes instructions as plain `AGENTS.md`, Codex is the tool that treats that file as the primary format.

**Skip it if** you want a polished editor experience or a flat price you can predict from the first day.

The Codex CLI install and a first command, as given in the `openai/codex` README and OpenAI's AGENTS.md guide:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://chatgpt.com/codex/install.sh | sh
codex --ask-for-approval never "Summarize the current instructions."
```

### Windsurf

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/windsurf.png" name="Windsurf" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** An AI editor whose pricing page is now titled "Devin Plans and Pricing" (windsurf.com's social image is hosted on devin.ai). The docs describe a "Devin Local" agent that shares a harness with the Devin CLI.

**Pricing (windsurf.com/pricing, November 26, 2026).** Free $0 with a light quota, limited models, and unlimited inline edits and Tab completions. Pro $20 a month with increased quotas and "frontier models from OpenAI, Claude, Gemini, and SpaceXAI." Max $200 a month with significantly higher quotas. Teams $80 a month, up to 100 users, with SAML/OIDC SSO. Paid plans have a usage allowance that "refreshes automatically on a daily and weekly basis."

**Pros**
- A real free tier with unlimited Tab and inline edits.
- Multiple model vendors on Pro.
- Customization list includes rules, skills, plugins, MCP servers and hooks, with `AGENTS.md` read as always-on rules.

**Cons**
- The brand and pricing page are in flux (Devin naming), which makes older reviews unreliable.
- Quotas are described qualitatively, not as numbers.

**Skip it if** you need published per-plan usage numbers or want a pure terminal workflow.

### GitHub Copilot

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/github-copilot.png" name="GitHub Copilot" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** GitHub's assistant across editors, the Copilot CLI, github.com and a cloud agent that opens pull requests.

**Pricing (github.com/features/copilot/plans, November 26, 2026).** Free $0 (limited chat and agent usage, with CLI and agent mode listed as included). Pro $10 a month. Pro+ $39. Max $100. Usage is now measured in GitHub AI Credits: the page lists base credits equal to the plan price (Pro $10, Pro+ $39, Max $100) plus flex allotments (Pro $5, Pro+ $31, Max $100). Pro+ and Max can delegate tasks to third-party coding agents such as Claude and Codex (marked Preview).

**Pros**
- Lowest paid entry among the big-brand options outside Cursor Plus.
- Works across many IDEs, the CLI and github.com, and reads `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, path-specific `.instructions.md` files, `AGENTS.md`, and a root `CLAUDE.md` or `GEMINI.md`.
- Pull request reviews and a cloud agent included from Pro.

**Cons**
- Credit accounting is new and more complex than a flat plan; flex allotments "may change over time."
- Pooled org-wide usage is not included on the individual plans.

**Skip it if** you want a single vendor's frontier model with a published multiplier, or you rarely touch GitHub.

### Gemini CLI

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/gemini-cli.png" name="Gemini CLI" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's open-source terminal agent (Apache 2.0, 109,187 GitHub stars as of November 26, 2026). The repository README has an important notice that many older posts miss: it is "designed for Gemini Code Assist Standard / Enterprise licenses," and free individual access and Google AI Pro and Ultra "moved to Antigravity CLI on June 18, 2026." It also says the CLI is not tuned for the Gemini 4 series.

**Pros**
- Open source, with a clear `GEMINI.md` context hierarchy and skills in `.gemini/skills/` or the `.agents/skills/` alias.
- Reasonable fit if your company is already a Gemini Code Assist customer.

**Cons**
- No individual free tier any more, per the README.
- The README itself points individuals and Gemini 4 users to a different tool (Antigravity CLI).

**Skip it if** you are an individual looking for a free Google agent. Use the Antigravity route the README describes, and see our sister-site comparison [Antigravity vs Claude Code](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/antigravity-vs-claude-code).

Install with npm or Homebrew:

```bash
npm install -g @google/gemini-cli
brew install gemini-cli
```

### Kiro

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/kiro.png" name="Kiro" size=40 /%}

{% figure src="https://kiro.dev/images/opengraph/home.png" alt="Kiro home page social image" caption="Kiro's home page image. Source: Kiro (kiro.dev)" /%}

**What it is.** An agentic IDE and agent from the Kiro team whose docs center on spec-driven work and "steering" files.

**Pricing (kiro.dev/pricing, November 26, 2026).** Free $0 with 50 credits a month and open-weight models (users who signed up with social login or AWS Builder ID also get Claude Sonnet 4.5). Pro $20, Pro+ $40, Pro Max $100, Power $200 (10,000 credits). Add-on credits cost $0.04 each. Paid plans list premium models including Auto, GPT-5.6 Sol, Claude Opus 5.5 and Grok 4.7, though "not all premium models are available in all countries/regions." First-time Pro upgraders get the rest of the month free plus 500 trial credits to use within 12 months. Teams and enterprises use credit pooling at $20 per seat per month with 500 included credits per seat.

**Pros**
- Free tier is perpetual, not a trial.
- Steering files in `.kiro/steering/` with global and workspace scope, and `AGENTS.md` support.
- Credit pooling suits teams with uneven usage.

**Cons**
- A credit is "a unit of work," and complex prompts cost more than one, so cost is hard to forecast.
- We did not verify Kiro's license or source availability.

**Skip it if** you want flat-rate unlimited usage.

### Zed

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-code-vs-cursor-2026/logos/zed.png" name="Zed" size=40 /%}

{% figure src="https://zed.dev/img/home/og.webp" alt="Zed editor home page social image" caption="Zed's home page image. Source: Zed (zed.dev)" /%}

**What it is.** A native, open-source code editor with an Agent Panel that can run Zed's hosted agent or external agents over ACP. As of November 26, 2026, the `zed-industries/zed` repository shows 95,788 GitHub stars (GitHub's license field reads NOASSERTION, and Zed's own pricing page says it is open source).

**Pricing (zed.dev/pricing, November 26, 2026).** Personal $0 forever (the editor without Zed-hosted AI, or bring your own keys and external agents). Pro $10 a month with unlimited edit predictions and $5 of tokens included; beyond that, usage is billed at API list price plus 10%. Business $30 per seat per month. A 14-day Pro trial with $5 of GPT-6 Luna usage requires no credit card.

**Pros**
- Cheap entry, an open-source editor, and the ability to bring your own agent.
- Honest token billing.

**Cons**
- You manage more yourself, and we did not verify Zed's rules or skills support for this post.

**Skip it if** you want a managed, all-in-one agent subscription.

## Pricing table (as of November 26, 2026)

Every figure below is a list price read from the vendor's own page on November 26, 2026, excluding tax. Plans change often, so treat this as a snapshot.

| Product | Plan | Price | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Claude Code | Pro | $20 monthly, or $17 per month billed annually ($200 up front) | Includes Claude Code |
| Claude Code | Max | $100 monthly | 5x Pro usage; billed monthly |
| Claude Code | Ultra | $200 monthly | 10x Pro weekly usage; no 5-hour limit for verified accounts |
| Cursor | Free | $0 | Limited |
| Cursor | Plus | $8 monthly ($80 annual) | Grok models only |
| Cursor | Premium | $30 monthly ($300 annual) | 4x Plus; cloud agents, Bugbot, third-party models at API list price |
| Cursor | Super | $100 monthly ($1,000 annual) | 15x Plus |
| Cursor | Ultra | $200 monthly ($2,000 annual) | 50x Plus |
| Cursor | Teams | From $30 per seat | Seat tiers vary; see cursor.com/pricing |
| Codex | Free | $0 | GPT-6 Luna, desktop app, subject to rollout |
| Codex | Pro | $100, $200 or $500 monthly | Ultrafast mode on the $500 tier |
| Windsurf | Pro / Max / Teams | $20 / $200 / $80 monthly | Teams up to 100 users |
| GitHub Copilot | Pro / Pro+ / Max | $10 / $39 / $100 monthly | Usage in GitHub AI Credits |
| Kiro | Pro / Pro+ / Pro Max / Power | $20 / $40 / $100 / $200 monthly | Add-on credits $0.04 each |
| Zed | Pro / Business | $10 monthly / $30 per seat | $5 of tokens included in Pro |
| Gemini CLI | Code Assist Standard or Enterprise | Not captured | Pricing is under the Google Cloud license, not on the repo |

A plain way to compare the two main tools: on annual billing, Claude Pro is $200 a year and Cursor Premium is $300 a year. At the top, Cursor Ultra is $2,000 a year on annual billing, while Claude Ultra at $200 a month is $2,400 over twelve months because Anthropic's table shows Max and Ultra as monthly-only. These are just arithmetic on the list prices. The usage you get per dollar is not comparable: Anthropic quotes multiples of Pro, Cursor quotes multiples of Plus, and neither publishes an absolute token count.

