## Head-to-head: Claude Code vs Cursor

{% callout type="info" %}
**Pick Claude Code** when the terminal is your home, when you want repo-committed memory, rules, hooks and skills with the most thorough documentation, and when one Claude subscription for chat plus coding is appealing. **Pick Cursor** when you want an editor, several model vendors, cloud agents and Bugbot reviews, or a team marketplace and admin controls. **Use both** if you like Cursor's editor and Claude Code's agent: Anthropic documents installing Claude Code in Cursor.
{% /callout %}

| Question | Claude Code | Cursor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Where do you work? | Terminal first; also IDE extensions, desktop and web | Editor first; also CLI, cloud agents and web or mobile handoff |
| Who owns it? | Anthropic | SpaceX (acquisition announced complete on August 14, 2026) |
| How do you pay? | Claude Pro, Max or Ultra subscription | The X Subscription tiers, or a legacy Cursor plan if you subscribed earlier |
| Free plan | No | Yes |
| Models | Anthropic's | Grok and Composer in the base pool; third-party models paid at API list price from Premium |
| Project memory | `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, auto memory | Rules (`.mdc`), `AGENTS.md`, User and Team Rules |
| Scoped rules | `.claude/rules/` | `globs` in `.cursor/rules/` |
| Skills | `.claude/skills/` | `.cursor/skills/`, `.agents/skills/`, and also `.claude/skills/` |
| Deterministic control | Hooks (documented as the way to block actions) | Hooks (`hooks.json`) |
| Review and CI | Scriptable CLI; code review is not listed on the pricing page | Print mode for CI; Bugbot reviews from Premium |
| Team admin | Team and Enterprise plans; organization-wide skills deployment is listed for Team and Enterprise, SCIM and audit logs for Enterprise | Team Rules with enforce toggle, marketplace, SSO, and SCIM on Enterprise |

**Our read.** The two tools have converged on the same primitives: markdown instructions, scoped rules, skills, hooks, MCP, subagents, a CLI and cloud execution. The differences that matter are now commercial and ergonomic. Claude Code gives you one vendor's models and one subscription, with the deepest documented customization surface. Cursor gives you an editor and a menu of models, but with a pricing structure that changed this year and an owner that now also sells you Grok. If you were burned by an AI editor's pricing change before, that structure is a legitimate reason to prefer the more predictable subscription. If you hate living in a terminal, no amount of memory-file elegance will fix that.

Conflicting information worth knowing: Cursor's pricing page and its help center agree on the plan table, but older Cursor pages and many third-party posts still describe Pro at $20 and Pro+ at $60. Cursor's help center confirms those are legacy plans, so we treat the pricing page as current and the older prices as legacy.

## Head-to-head: Claude Code vs Codex

Both are agents that started in the terminal and both have open instruction conventions. Codex's CLI is Apache 2.0 licensed and its `AGENTS.md` discovery is the most explicit we read: a global file in `~/.codex`, a walk from the Git root to the working directory, one file per directory, a root-first merge, and a default 32 KiB cap. Claude Code's `CLAUDE.md` loading is similarly layered but adds `@` imports, `.claude/rules/` and auto memory. On price, Codex rides on ChatGPT plans, including a free tier, while Claude Code requires a paid plan. If you want the closest like-for-like review of terminal agents, our sister site's [Claude Code vs Codex vs Gemini CLI vs Copilot CLI](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/claude-code-vs-codex-vs-gemini-cli-vs-copilot-cli-2026) covers that matchup with a different angle, and our own note on [Codex usage limits](https://agentskills.codes/blog/codex-usage-limits-agent-workflows) looks at how its limits work.

**Which to pick:** choose Codex if you want an open-source CLI and a free way in. Choose Claude Code if the surrounding ecosystem (hooks, subagents, skills, remote control) is what you want.

## Head-to-head: Cursor vs Windsurf

Both are editors with agents, both sell tiers at $20 and $200 (Windsurf Pro and Max, versus Cursor's legacy Pro and current Ultra), and both read `AGENTS.md` and `SKILL.md`. Cursor's current individual ladder is more granular ($8, $30, $100, $200) and ties into X and Grok; Windsurf's is $20, $200 for individuals and $80 for Teams, and its pricing page is now branded "Devin." Windsurf Free explicitly includes unlimited Tab completions and inline edits. Cursor documents cloud agents, Bugbot and a team marketplace more fully on its pricing page.

**Which to pick:** Windsurf if you want a simple three-tier ladder and a free tier with unlimited Tab. Cursor if you need Bugbot, cloud agents and the finer-grained ladder.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

Skills written as `SKILL.md` folders are the shared layer between Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf and Gemini CLI, so the catalog at agentskills.codes is useful whichever of these you pick. We checked that each page below returns HTTP 200 on November 26, 2026. Install counts come from the catalog's API on the same day and are small, so treat them as a rough signal only.

| Catalog entry | Why it fits this comparison |
| --- | --- |
| [agents-md](https://agentskills.codes/skills/agents-md) | Creates or updates root and nested `AGENTS.md` files for scoped conventions, the one file Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Copilot and Claude Code (without a `CLAUDE.md`) all read. |
| [sync-agents-md](https://agentskills.codes/skills/sync-agents-md) | Re-syncs `AGENTS.md` after a repo has drifted, which matters when two tools share the same instruction file. |
| [claude-code-guide](https://agentskills.codes/skills/claude-code-guide) | A guide for using Claude Code with configuration templates and prompting strategies. |
| [cursor-codebase-indexing](https://agentskills.codes/skills/cursor-codebase-indexing) | Covers setting up and optimizing Cursor's codebase indexing. |
| [cursor-explorer-mcp](https://agentskills.codes/skills/cursor-explorer-mcp) | An MCP-oriented skill for token-expensive multi-file analysis and codebase exploration. |
| [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) | A guide for building MCP servers, which both Claude Code and Cursor can connect to (136 listed installs when we checked). |
| [mcp-integration](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-integration) | For adding and configuring MCP servers in an agent setup (21 listed installs). |
| [playwright-mcp](https://agentskills.codes/skills/playwright-mcp) | Browser testing and UI validation through Playwright MCP, useful when the agent needs to check what it built (33 listed installs). |

Related reading on this site: [AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md vs SKILL.md vs Cursor rules](https://agentskills.codes/blog/agents-md-vs-claude-md-vs-skill-md-vs-cursor-rules), [Best Claude Code skills for developers in 2026](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026) and [Best free AI coding agents in 2026](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-free-ai-coding-agents-2026).

To try a skill in Claude Code, a personal skill lives under `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`; a project skill lives under `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`. Because Cursor also reads `.claude/skills/`, committing a skill there serves both tools.

## Best for X: decision table

| If you... | Pick | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Live in the terminal and want hooks and subagents | Claude Code | Documented memory, rules, hooks, subagents and skills in one product |
| Want an editor with several model vendors | Cursor | Third-party models at API list price from Premium |
| Need a free way to try an agent | Copilot Free, Kiro Free, Windsurf Free, Codex Free or Cursor Free | Each lists a $0 plan; Claude Code does not |
| Want the cheapest paid plan | Cursor Plus ($8, Grok only), then Copilot Pro or Zed Pro ($10) | List prices on November 26, 2026 |
| Want an open-source agent CLI | Codex (Apache 2.0) | Source is public; Gemini CLI is also Apache 2.0 but is now enterprise-oriented |
| Run a team with admin and review needs | Cursor Teams or Enterprise | Marketplace, Team Rules, Bugbot, SSO, SCIM |
| Already pay for ChatGPT | Codex | Included in the plan |
| Already pay for Claude | Claude Code | Included in Pro, Max and Ultra |
| Want spec-driven steering and pooled team credits | Kiro | Steering files and credit pooling |
| Want a fast native editor and your own agent | Zed | Open-source editor with external agents via ACP |

## Common mistakes

1. **Quoting old Cursor prices.** Pro at $20 and Pro+ at $60 are legacy plans for existing subscribers; new customers see Free, Plus, Premium, Super and Ultra.
2. **Buying Cursor Plus and expecting Claude or GPT models.** The pricing page says only Grok models are available on Plus.
3. **Forgetting that third-party models in Cursor are billed separately.** The pricing page says that usage "must be purchased separately."
4. **Expecting Claude Code on the free Claude plan.** Anthropic's table shows it as not included.
5. **Keeping both `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` without an import.** Claude Code reads only the `CLAUDE.md` files when both exist, so instructions silently diverge. Add `@AGENTS.md` to the `CLAUDE.md`.
6. **Writing Cursor rules as `.md` files.** Project rules must be `.mdc`; a plain `.md` in `.cursor/rules` is ignored.
7. **Treating a rule as a guarantee.** For anything safety-critical, use a hook.
8. **Installing Gemini CLI as an individual and expecting a free tier.** Its README says free individual access moved to Antigravity CLI.
9. **Ignoring that Claude usage is one pool.** Chat, desktop, mobile and Claude Code share it.
10. **Overlong instruction files.** Anthropic suggests under 200 lines for CLAUDE.md and Cursor suggests under 500 lines per rule.

## Buying and setup checklist

- [ ] Decide terminal-first or editor-first, then choose Claude Code or Cursor.
- [ ] Check the live pricing pages (links in Sources) on the day you buy; plans change.
- [ ] If you pick Cursor, confirm whether you are buying a current plan or already hold a legacy one.
- [ ] If you pick Claude Code, confirm your plan includes it (Pro and above) and how your team shares usage.
- [ ] Write one `AGENTS.md` for shared conventions; add a `CLAUDE.md` that imports it if you use Claude Code.
- [ ] Put skills in `.claude/skills/` (works in both Claude Code and Cursor) or symlink from `.agents/skills/`.
- [ ] Turn any must-never-happen rule into a hook.
- [ ] Connect only the MCP servers you need; each adds context cost.
- [ ] Try the free tier of a second tool before locking in an annual plan.

## How we evaluated this

**What we did.** On November 26, 2026 we read the vendor pages listed in Sources: pricing pages for Claude, Cursor, Windsurf, Kiro, Zed, GitHub Copilot and Codex; Claude Code docs on overview, memory, skills, hooks, subagents, MCP and best practices; Cursor docs on rules, skills, hooks, MCP, CLI and the agent; Windsurf, Gemini CLI, Kiro and GitHub Copilot documentation for instruction files and skills; and Cursor's SpaceX announcement and billing help page. We rendered Cursor's pricing page in a browser because its raw text mixed in X features. We queried the GitHub API for star counts and licenses. We checked the YouTube video with oEmbed, each X post with the syndication endpoint, every catalog and blog link with an HTTP request, and every image with a `curl -I` request.

**What we did not do.** We did not install or run Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf, Copilot, Gemini CLI, Kiro or Zed for this post, and we did not run benchmarks. No neutral benchmark we could verify compares Claude Code and Cursor directly, so we make no claims about code quality or speed. We did not read Reddit threads, so this post does not summarize community opinion. We did not capture Claude Team seat pricing or Codex Plus pricing reliably, and we say so where relevant.

## FAQ

### Is Claude Code better than Cursor?
Neither is better in general. Claude Code is a terminal-first agent bundled with Claude plans; Cursor is an editor-centered product with cloud agents and several model vendors. Pick by where you want to work and how you want to pay. Neither vendor publishes a neutral head-to-head benchmark that we could verify, so we do not rank them on output quality.

### Can I use Claude Code inside Cursor?
Yes, in the sense that Anthropic's Claude Code docs include an "Install for Cursor" link for its VS Code extension. Cursor also reads `.claude/skills/` and can load hooks from third-party tools such as Claude Code, per Cursor's docs. We did not test the combination ourselves.

### How much does Claude Code cost in November 2026?
Claude Code is included in Claude Pro ($20 monthly, or $17 per month billed annually), Max ($100) and Ultra ($200), per claude.com/pricing on November 26, 2026. The Free plan does not include it. Team and Enterprise plans exist; we did not capture their seat prices.

### How much does Cursor cost in November 2026?
The current plans are Free, Plus ($8), Premium ($30), Super ($100), Ultra ($200) and Teams (from $30 per seat), per cursor.com/pricing on November 26, 2026. Plus allows Grok models only. Third-party model usage is billed separately. Legacy plans such as Pro at $20 continue for existing subscribers.

### Who owns Cursor now?
Cursor's blog says it was acquired by SpaceX, announced complete on August 14, 2026. Its help center says Cursor is now part of The X Subscription. Treat the acquisition details beyond those two pages as unverified by us.

### Is there a free version of Claude Code?
No. Anthropic's pricing table shows Claude Code as not included on Free. If you want a free agent to try, Copilot Free, Kiro Free, Windsurf Free, Codex on ChatGPT Free and Cursor Free all list a $0 plan, with different limits.

### Does Cursor support AGENTS.md and skills?
Yes. Cursor's docs say it supports `AGENTS.md` in the project root and subdirectories, and Agent Skills in `.cursor/skills/` and `.agents/skills/`, plus `.claude/skills/` and `.codex/skills/` for compatibility.

### Does Claude Code read AGENTS.md?
Yes, from v2.1.277 or later, but only when there is no `CLAUDE.md` in your working directory or above it. If both exist, Claude reads the `CLAUDE.md` files only, unless the `CLAUDE.md` imports `AGENTS.md`.

### What is the difference between rules and skills?
Rules are instructions that load on a condition (always, by glob, by description or when you mention them). Skills are folders with a `SKILL.md` and optional scripts that the agent loads when relevant or when you invoke them with a slash command. Both Claude Code and Cursor document each.

### What do people on Reddit say about Claude Code vs Cursor?
We did not read or verify Reddit threads for this post, so we do not summarize them. One caution when you read them: a thread from before August 2026 may quote Cursor prices that are now legacy.

### Should I use Claude Code or Cursor for a team?
Cursor's pricing page lists team-oriented features (marketplace for internal rules, skills and plugins, Team Rules, SSO, centralized billing, and on Enterprise SCIM and audit logs). Anthropic's page lists Team and Enterprise plans, with organization-wide skills deployment shown for both and SCIM and audit logs on Enterprise. Compare both admin pages against your compliance needs.

### Are Windsurf, Codex, Copilot, Gemini CLI, Kiro and Zed worth considering?
Yes, for specific reasons: Windsurf for its free Tab and a three-tier ladder, Codex for an open-source CLI and a ChatGPT-bundled plan, Copilot for the lowest paid entry and GitHub integration, Kiro for steering files and credits, and Zed for an open-source native editor. Gemini CLI is now aimed at Gemini Code Assist customers, per its README.

### Will these prices change?
Probably. Cursor's pricing structure changed this year, Gemini CLI's free access moved to a different tool in June, and Windsurf's page is now branded Devin. Re-check the vendor page before paying.

